course: biology
lesson: 63
title: Microbes: bacteria and viruses, good and bad
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Microbes are living things far too small to see; a thousand bacteria in a row would just about stretch across a pinhead.
explain: Put a drop of pond water under the school microscope and a hidden zoo appears: wriggling, spinning, tiny creatures that were there all along.
explain: Most microbes are harmless or helpful: they make yoghurt and cheese, rot dead things, and trillions of them live in your gut helping to digest food.
explain: A few are germs that make us ill, and viruses are stranger still: much smaller than bacteria, they cannot do anything on their own and only copy themselves inside living cells.
explain: We fight bad germs with soap and water, vaccines that train the body, antibiotics for bacteria, and a fever that turns up the body's heat while it fights.
fact: Microbes are living things too small to see with the eye alone; a typical bacterium is about 1 to 2 thousandths of a millimetre long.
fact: The Dutch cloth merchant Antonie van Leeuwenhoek made tiny powerful lenses and was among the first to see microbes; in 1683 he described bacteria from scrapings of his own teeth.
fact: The human gut holds around 40 trillion bacteria, about as many as the body's own cells, and most of them are helpful.
fact: Yoghurt is milk changed by friendly bacteria, which eat the milk sugar and make lactic acid that thickens the milk and makes it tangy; «live cultures» on a pot means these friendly bacteria are still in it.
fact: Soap breaks up the greasy layer that germs cling to and running water rinses them away; washing for about 20 seconds gives it time to work; cold viruses spread on hands and on things people touch, like door handles.
fact: A cold is caused by a virus; a virus is much smaller than a bacterium and can only copy itself inside a living cell.
fact: Antibiotics kill bacteria or stop them growing but do nothing to viruses, so they do not help colds or flu, and using them when not needed helps bacteria become resistant; stopping a course of antibiotics early can leave the toughest bacteria alive to grow back.
fact: A vaccine shows the body a harmless copy or piece of a germ, so the immune system learns to recognise it and can fight the real germ fast; Edward Jenner made the first vaccine, against smallpox, in 1796.
fact: Smallpox, once one of the deadliest diseases, was wiped out from the whole world by vaccination, and this was declared in 1980: so many people were protected that the virus had nowhere left to spread.
fact: A fever means the body has turned up its thermostat to around 38 to 39 degrees instead of about 37, which helps the immune system fight germs.
fact: Plaque is a sticky film of bacteria on teeth; they feed on sugar and make acid that slowly dissolves the enamel and makes holes; a dentist's pink disclosing tablet colours the plaque you missed brushing.
fact: Some microbes live in extreme places, such as boiling hot springs; the bright rings of Grand Prismatic Spring in Yellowstone are mats of different microbes living at different temperatures, showing that life survives in places once thought impossible.
fact: Milk left out on a warm table goes sour because bacteria multiply fast in warmth and make acid; the fridge slows them down.
exercise: About how long is a typical bacterium? | About 1 to 2 thousandths of a millimetre | About 1 millimetre, like sand | About 1 centimetre, like an ant | About as long as a hair is long
exercise: What can you see in pond water under a school microscope? | Tiny living creatures too small to see by eye | Nothing at all, it is empty | Only grains of sand and dust | Baby fish that have just hatched
exercise: Who was among the first people to see bacteria, in his own tooth scrapings? | Antonie van Leeuwenhoek | Alexander Fleming, with penicillin | Charles Darwin, on his ship voyage | Isaac Newton, with his telescope
exercise: About how many bacteria live in a person's gut? | Around 40 trillion | Around 40, all of one kind | Around 40 thousand or so | None, the gut is clean
exercise: How do bacteria turn milk into yoghurt? | They eat the milk sugar and make lactic acid that thickens it | They add tiny bits of fruit to it | They freeze it until it goes thick and sets like ice cream in a tub | They suck the water out of it
exercise: Why should you wash your hands with soap for about 20 seconds? | Soap needs time to break up the grease germs cling to | It takes 20 seconds for the water to get hot | Germs fall asleep after 20 seconds | Soap only kills germs after 20 seconds of drying in the warm air
exercise: What causes a common cold? | A virus | A bacterium | Getting cold | Eating ice cream
exercise: How is a virus different from a bacterium? | It is much smaller and can only copy itself inside living cells | It is much bigger and lives only in water | It is a kind of tiny insect | It is a plant that grows in the nose
exercise: Why won't antibiotics help a cold? | Antibiotics work on bacteria, and colds are caused by viruses | Colds are far too strong for any medicine | Antibiotics only work in summer | Antibiotics only help broken bones
exercise: How does a vaccine protect you? | It trains the immune system with a harmless copy or piece of a germ | It kills every germ in your body for ever, good and bad alike, in a day | It puts a shield of metal around your cells | It makes you too hot for germs to live
exercise: What was the first vaccine made against? | Smallpox, by Edward Jenner in 1796 | Colds, by Louis Pasteur in about 1900 | Toothache, by a dentist in 1950 | Hay fever, by a doctor in 1850
exercise: What is a fever? | The body turning up its thermostat to fight germs | The body breaking down because it is far too hot outside | A germ that lives in the forehead | Sweat that has turned warm
exercise: What is normal body temperature? | About 37 degrees | About 30 degrees, like a summer day | About 42 degrees, like a hot bath | About 20 degrees, like a cool room
exercise: How does plaque make holes in teeth? | Its bacteria feed on sugar and make acid that dissolves enamel | Its bacteria chew holes in the teeth with tiny teeth of their own | It freezes the teeth until they crack | It pulls the teeth out of the gums
exercise: What makes the bright colours in Yellowstone's hot springs? | Mats of different microbes living at different temperatures | Paint added for tourists | Coloured rocks slowly melting in the boiling heat of the water | Rainbows reflected from the sky
exercise: Why does milk go sour on a warm table? | Bacteria multiply quickly in warmth and make acid | The milk dries out in the air | Sunlight turns the milk into cheese | The cow's milk was already too old when it came out of the cow
exercise: Why does keeping milk in the fridge make it last longer? | The cold slows down the bacteria | The cold kills every bacterium at once | The fridge adds extra fresh milk to it | The cold makes the milk much thicker
test: are germs all bad?? my teacher said u need some | No, most microbes are harmless or helpful, like those in your gut | Yes, every microbe makes you ill | Yes, but only the ones in water | No, germs are all good and not a single one of them can ever harm you
test: just give me antibiotics for this cold doc, I've got a match saturday | They won't help: a cold is a virus, and antibiotics work on bacteria | Fine, they cure any cold in a day | Only if you take double | Yes, but only with orange juice
test: What would happen if everyone stopped washing their hands before meals? | More germs would spread and more people would get ill | Nothing, germs cannot live on hands | Everyone would become immune at once and never get ill again | Food would taste better
test: In a cartoon, a germ is a green monster as big as a football that you can punch. Put it right. | Real germs are far too small to see without a microscope | Germs are as big as cats | Germs are visible as a cloud of green smoke when you sneeze | Germs can be caught with a net
test: Tom says his fever is the illness itself, so it must be cooled to zero at once. Right? | No, a fever is the body turning up its heat to help fight germs | Yes, a fever is a germ that lives in the head | Yes, body heat itself is a disease | No, a fever means you are getting better already and it will stop soon
test: Why was smallpox the first disease to be wiped out from the whole world? | Vaccines protected so many that the virus had nowhere left to spread | Doctors found and killed every smallpox germ with soap | Smallpox germs died of old age | The disease moved to animals only
test: A friend sneezes into his hand, then grabs the door handle. What has probably happened? | Cold viruses may now be on the handle for the next person | The viruses died as soon as they left his nose | The handle has made the viruses stronger | Nothing, colds only spread through cold winter air, not on hands
test: A dentist gives Ana a pink tablet to chew and her teeth go pink in spots. What do the pink spots show? | Plaque, the sticky film of bacteria she missed brushing | Holes that are already in her teeth | Places where her teeth are strongest | Food colouring left on her teeth from her breakfast cereal
test: the yoghurt says live cultures on it, is it gone off? | No, live cultures are the friendly bacteria that made the yoghurt | Yes, it is full of bad germs | Yes, it means it is alive and will grow out of the pot if you leave it | No, it means it was made yesterday
test: Some microbes live in boiling hot springs. What does that tell scientists? | Life can survive in places once thought impossible | Hot water is the only place life can exist | All microbes love boiling water | Hot springs make new microbes out of the rocks
test: Why must you finish a course of antibiotics as the doctor says? | Stopping early can leave tougher bacteria that may grow back | Unfinished pills explode in the box | Your body needs the rest as food | It makes the medicine taste much better next time you are ill
test: Leeuwenhoek wrote about «little animals» in his tooth scrapings. Why did people not see them before? | Nobody had lenses powerful enough to see microbes | Microbes did not exist before 1683 | People were not allowed to look at teeth before then | Microbes only lived in Dutch mouths
exam: Identify the principal reason for which antibiotic medication is ineffective against the common cold. | The common cold is caused by a virus, upon which antibiotics have no effect | The cold virus is too large for antibiotics to penetrate | Antibiotics act only on fungi | The common cold is not an infection of any kind, being caused by low temperature
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mode of action of a vaccine? | It exposes the immune system to a harmless form or part of a pathogen | It destroys all microorganisms within the body, both harmful and benign | It raises the body's temperature permanently | It replaces the immune system entirely
exam: It is given that milk is left at room temperature for one day. Ascertain the principal cause of its souring. | The multiplication of bacteria producing acid | The evaporation of water from the milk | The action of light upon the fat | The absorption of oxygen by the sugar
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the bacteria of the human gut. | They number some tens of trillions and are mostly beneficial | They are few in number and wholly harmful | They are present only during illness | They are identical to viruses in their size and in their manner of life
